---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Mark from '../components/Mark.astro'

const city = [
  ['Artemis', 'Patron deity', 'The orchestrator. The one agent you speak to — routes work, adjudicates, escalates only what needs you.'],
  ['Hermes', 'The messenger', 'Mailboxes, delivery, speech-act messages, anti-livelock rules.'],
  ['The Agora', 'The marketplace', 'Shared coordination on disk. A git repository with a single committer.'],
  ['The Library', 'Library of Celsus', 'Per-agent memory and semantic recall, with reflection so it never grows unbounded.'],
  ['The Odeon', 'Council theatre', 'Briefings, slide reviews, decision memos, live meetings.'],
  ['The Terraces', 'Terrace houses', 'The floor. Every agent an avatar at a desk — watchability as observability.'],
  ['The Watch', 'City walls', 'Human gates, budgets, the circuit breaker, the secret broker.'],
  ['The Gymnasium', 'Training grounds', 'The self-improvement loop: observe, propose, gate, land, measure.'],
  ['The Stoa', 'Colonnade of scholars', 'Research. Studies registered repositories, files provenance-cited briefs.']
]

const figures = [
  ['327', 'commits'],
  ['26', 'decision records'],
  ['197', 'test files'],
  ['106', 'documents'],
  ['53', 'implementations']
]

const strip = [
  ['Status', 'pre-alpha'],
  ['Installer', 'none yet'],
  ['Engine', 'claude'],
  ['Milestone', 'M8'],
  ['Licence', 'MIT']
]
---

<Base
  title="Ephesus — an agent company you govern as its architect"
  description="A desktop harness that turns the claude CLI into a self-coordinating company of agents with memory, mailboxes, and desks on a 2D floor. Pre-alpha, MIT, built in the open.">

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hgrid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Pre-alpha · MIT · built in the open</span>
        <h1>An agent company you govern as its architect</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A desktop harness that turns the <code>claude</code> CLI you already pay for into a
          self-coordinating company of agents — each with memory, a mailbox, and a desk on a
          two-dimensional floor. You describe intent to Artemis. The company decomposes it,
          assigns it, and reports back.
        </p>
        <div class="btnrow">
          <a class="btn solid" href="/contribute">Start contributing</a>
          <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus">Read the source</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hmark"><Mark size={200} /></div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="strip">
        {strip.map(([k, v]) => <span><b>{k}</b>{v}</span>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Honest status</span>
      <h2>Not finished, and not pretending to be</h2>
      <p>
        There are no releases and no installer. Signed builds are a planned milestone and have not
        shipped. If you want to <em>use</em> Ephesus, the honest answer is: not yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to <em>build</em> it, everything runs from source today — and this is the
        interesting moment to arrive, because every decision is written down before it is made.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One engine today.</strong> The engine layer is an adapter seam by design and others
        are planned, but <code>claude</code> is what is wired and tested, so it is what this page
        claims.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">The city</span>
      <h2>Every subsystem is named for the part of the city that did its job</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:26px">
        Ephesus was a working city — a harbour that moved goods, an agora where business was
        coordinated, a library that held its knowledge. The software takes the same shape.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Subsystem</th><th>In the city</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {city.map(([a, b, c]) => (
              <tr><td>{a}</td><td style="color:var(--ink-3)">{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="btnrow"><a class="btn" href="/city">The full architecture</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">The method</span>
      <h2>A claim without evidence is not done</h2>
      <p>
        Most of what is unusual here is not a feature. It is a rule about proof, applied to the
        system and to its own construction without distinction.
      </p>
      <blockquote>The recurring defect of this codebase is a check that cannot fail.</blockquote>
      <p>
        That sentence was written after five separate instances were found in a single day: a
        coverage figure that rose while the wiring stayed untested; a matcher tested only against
        strings we had written ourselves; a suite reporting green over 1,406 lines that nothing
        could reach.
      </p>
      <p>
        What followed is mechanical rather than cultural. A wiring seam with no test is a defect
        rather than a gap. Every production module must be reachable from an application entry
        point. An engine adapter may not match on output this repository has never observed. Every
        degradation is visible in the interface, never a silent fallback.
      </p>
      <div class="btnrow"><a class="btn" href="/blog">Read the writing</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">The record</span>
      <h2>Eleven days, written down</h2>
      <div class="figs">
        {figures.map(([n, l]) => <div class="fig"><b>{n}</b><span>{l}</span></div>)}
      </div>
      <p class="asof">Counted from the repository on 5 September 2026.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:20px">
        Every milestone closes on a run, not a checklist, and is audited by two independent passes —
        one verifying by execution, one checking conformance to the design.
      </p>
      <div class="btnrow"><a class="btn" href="/record">The build record</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Help wanted</span>
      <h2>The most useful thing you can do today</h2>
      <p>
        Ephesus has been built and exercised almost entirely on Windows. CI runs the suite on Linux,
        but nothing has ever launched the application there. <strong>Clone it, run it on a machine
        that is not Windows, and open an issue about the first thing that goes wrong.</strong>
      </p>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus.git
cd Ephesus
npm install
npm run dev</code></pre>
      <div class="btnrow">
        <a class="btn solid" href="https://github.com/Cassandra-Collective/Ephesus/labels/good%20first%20issue">Good first issues</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/contribute">How to contribute</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .hero { padding: 66px 0 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-3); }
    .hgrid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.55fr 1fr; gap: 44px; align-items: center; }
    .hmark { display: flex; justify-content: center; }
    .hmark svg { box-shadow: 5px 5px 0 0 var(--ink); }
    @media (max-width: 860px) {
      .hgrid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .hmark { display: none; }
    }
    .strip {
      display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; border: 2px solid var(--ink);
      margin: 44px 0 0; background: #FBF9F4;
    }
    .strip span {
      font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; color: var(--ink-2); padding: 11px 16px;
      border-right: 1px solid var(--paper-3); flex: 1; min-width: 150px;
    }
    .strip span:last-child { border-right: 0; }
    .strip b {
      display: block; font-size: 9.5px; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-3); font-weight: 400; margin-bottom: 3px;
    }
    .figs {
      display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(150px, 1fr));
      border: 2px solid var(--ink); background: #FBF9F4;
    }
    .fig { padding: 20px 18px; border-right: 1px solid var(--paper-3); }
    .fig:last-child { border-right: 0; }
    .fig b {
      display: block; font-family: var(--pixel); font-size: 22px;
      color: var(--terracotta); line-height: 1.4;
    }
    .asof {
      font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--ink-3); margin-top: 12px;
    }
    .fig span {
      display: block; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .12em;
      text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); margin-top: 10px;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
